---
// src/pages/blue-staffy-uk-breeders/index.astro — REBUILT from the approved board record
// (data/boards/blue-staffy-uk-breeders.json, approved 2026-09-20). Hand-written and NOT
// regenerated by `npm run extract`: the slug is listed in data/facts/rebuilt.json, so
// `migration_parity.py` skips it and `facts_preserved_check.py`, `link_parity_check.py` and
// `verbatim_set_check.py` are its gates instead.
//
// THIS IS THE ABOUT PAGE. Its question is "who am I dealing with", so it leads on the person
// — Lisa Bright, Carlisle · Cumbria — and then evidences each claim in turn: the two named
// DNA clearances on the two named parents, what "raised in our home" actually consists of,
// what Kennel Club registration covers and what it does not, and how a puppy gets from here
// to a doorstep. The record's strategy block calls that "the evidenced breeder".
//
// NO YEARS AND NO SCHEME. The migrated body carried no year, no affix and no line older than
// Maggie and Jones, so this page states none: no "years breeding", no "since <year>", no
// Assured Breeder scheme, no licence number, no guarantee length (data/settings.json holds
// `guarantee_days: null`) and no customer count. The two claims the migrated page made that
// no file on disk holds — "hundreds of families" and "lifelong support" — are struck by name
// in the record's `dropped.text` and in `verbatim.changed`, and neither is restated here.
//
// WORKING RULE 15 (faithful rewrite). The VERBATIM SET — the migrated H1, every H2/H3
// carrying a target keyword, the first paragraph under each of those headings, the FAQ
// questions and every image alt — is carried WORD FOR WORD, and the carried wording is READ
// OFF THE RECORD (`sections[].heading`, `sections[].verbatim_opening`, the tree nodes' own
// pair, `assets[].alt`, and the `data/faq.json` rows the record names) rather than retyped
// here, so a curly quote cannot drift between the record the breeder approved and the page
// that ships. Twenty deviations are recorded in `verbatim.changed` with their reasons. THE H1
// IS ONE OF THEM: `h1.pick` is variant 0, which is the migrated H1 with the clause naming the
// former city taken out (Known Issue 16), and the record already carries that row. So the
// slug's keyword phrase sits in the hero EYEBROW rather than in the H1 — once — and again in
// the migrated H2 the `our-story` section carries word for word.
//
// WORKING RULE 16. The hero is `H-AB1` — a photo mosaic LEFT of the copy on a steel band,
// with a quote in the aside — and the counter strip is `C-AB1`, figure cards on a steel band
// beside the heading. Both are the about family's own arrangements, resolved through
// `pickedStyle()`, and neither is one any for-sale page took. That hero is also what keeps
// this page's tuple apart from /privacy-policy-uk/'s: the interior-utility heroes all carry
// `ledge: 'none'`.
//
// NOTHING IN THE ASIDE IS WRITTEN HERE AND NOTHING IN IT IS UNSOURCED. The quote is the
// record's `sections[top].hero.aside.quote`, which is now the THIRD SENTENCE of the migrated
// page's own opening paragraph — a pull quote out of the paragraph this page renders in full
// under the H3 below — and the build asserts that containment rather than trusting it. The
// sentence the record was approved with read as Lisa's and appeared in no file on disk; it is
// replaced and re-approved (spec §9 amendment 11). The record's three CHIPS are rendered in
// the same box, as one credential line: `Hero` shows a chip row only for `ledge: 'chips'`, so
// on H-AB1 the prop is handed over and dropped, and a claim the breeder approved is not worth
// losing to an arrangement. The three TICKS are the one thing the box cannot hold — measured,
// they are 60-80px past the 450px ceiling at 1024 — and all three are stated in the paragraph
// under the hero instead. Both are still PASSED to the component, so a re-pick of H-AB2 or
// H-AB3, whose ledge is `ticks`, renders them the component's way with no page edit.
//
// THREE FIGURES, ALL COUNTED AT BUILD (amendment 10.5, and the lesson da9c34e wrote down).
// The record's `n` is a hand-written number — true on the day it was boarded — so each tile
// is derived from the file its `source` names (the health board's DNA table, the length of
// data/puppies.json, the two delivery settings) and a count that no longer matches what the
// breeder approved throws with both numbers in the message. The hero's delivery tick is the
// same claim as the third tile, so it is rebuilt from the same two settings and found by its
// recorded source rather than by its position.
//
// THE EMBED IS CARRIED AT ITS ORIGINAL ID (working rule 14, spec §9 amendment 7).
// `fXhu9jDS6CA` is the one id data/facts records for this page; it is mounted through
// `VideoEmbed` in the record's own `video-our-home` section, at the foot of the page where
// the migrated body had it, in the picked S1 arrangement — the player in a card.
//
// SIX SECTIONS CARRY NO PICK. `our-story`, `health-testing`, `home-raising`, `kennel-club`,
// `delivery` and `welcome` have `options.pick: null` on this record, so no arrangement was
// ever chosen for them and none is invented: they render with no `bl-box` class at all,
// exactly as /buy-staffy-puppies-for-sale-uk/'s two no-pick sections do. `pickedStyle()` is
// never called for them, because an arrangement nobody chose is not a smaller defect than a
// missing class.
//
// SCHEMA IS AboutPage + FAQPage, AND THE ORGANIZATION IS THE SITEWIDE ONE
// (`brief.schema.types`, `offer_model: "none"`). Every page already carries the `#business`
// node from `Schema.astro` — a LocalBusiness, which IS an Organization, built from
// data/settings.json with a TOWN-LEVEL address and no geo (Known Issue 16). One minted here
// would be a second identity for the same kennel, which `schema_check.py` reports and which
// would be false besides, so the AboutPage node points AT that node instead. No offer of any
// kind: the listing owns the six per-puppy Offers and the price page owns the AggregateOffer.
//
// H4-H6 ARE WRITTEN HERE, not in the record (amendment 2.1): the approved outline stops at
// the H3 the breeder read. Every ladder sentence traces to the migrated body or to a file
// under data/, and the H6 lines all open on the record's own `tuple.h6_prefixes` entry,
// "Breeder Note:". Every heading renders through `titleCase()` (amendment 4b).
import PageShell from '../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/kit/Hero.astro';
import CounterStrip from '../../components/kit/CounterStrip.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../../components/kit/VideoEmbed.astro';
import PuppyCard from '../../components/kit/PuppyCard.astro';
import Testimonial from '../../components/kit/Testimonial.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/kit/Faq.astro';
import { sectionsFromRecord } from '../../lib/sections';
import { pickedStyle } from '../../lib/pickedStyle';
import { bakedAssets, bakedSrcset, inPublic, BODY_SIZES } from '../../lib/assets';
import { recordText, countedFigures, ledgeText, sourceKey, type LedgeEntry } from '../../lib/recordText';
import { boxClass } from '../../lib/boardStyles';
import { titleCase as T } from '../../lib/headings';
import { loadFaq } from '../../lib/faq';
import { SITE, abs, SITE_URL, type PuppyRow } from '../../lib/site';
import { videoObject } from '../../lib/video';
import puppiesJson from '../../../data/puppies.json';
import reviewsJson from '../../../data/reviews.json';
import settings from '../../../data/settings.json';
import healthRecord from '../../../data/boards/blue-staffy-health-uk.json';
import record from '../../../data/boards/blue-staffy-uk-breeders.json';

const path = '/blue-staffy-uk-breeders/';
// The picked meta variants and H1, BY INDEX from the approval — never retyped here.
const title = record.meta_set.titles[record.approval!.meta!.title];
const description = record.meta_set.descriptions[record.approval!.meta!.description];
const h1 = record.h1.variants[record.approval!.h1];

// The seven migrated photographs plus the two optional hero tiles, by slot, at their ORIGINAL
// public paths with the alt text the record baked (working rule 11). The two tiles point at
// files this page also renders full size in their own sections, with different wording —
// which `img-alt-present-and-unique` requires and which rule 15 reads as a SET of alts per
// src (the fix 3fa824b made). A missing slot throws at build.
const asset = bakedAssets(record);
const familyDog = asset('about-family-dog');
const heroTile2 = asset('about-hero-tile-2');
const heroTile3 = asset('about-hero-tile-3');
const breederHome = asset('about-breeder-home');
const playfulLawn = asset('about-playful-lawn');
const testimonialDavid = asset('about-testimonial-david');
const familyFriendly = asset('about-family-friendly');
const deliveryVan = asset('about-nationwide-delivery');
const trustedFamily = asset('about-trusted-family');

// THE LIVE LITTER, filtered on `status` rather than hard-coded: a puppy leaving the list is a
// data edit, not a page edit. The cards' own links are grid data and are exempt from link
// parity (amendment 6b). No per-puppy Offer is emitted here — the listing owns them.
const available = (puppiesJson as PuppyRow[]).filter((p) => p.status === 'Available');

// THE REVIEWS, BY NAME rather than by index. `paperwork-proof` is MODE: single on Mark J,
// which the record's intent states inside `record_hash` — he is the only one of the three who
// names the paperwork and the health testing, which is exactly what the section above him
// claims. `families` is MODE: grid on all three. Entry 2 appears in both, deliberately, and
// the record says so. Nothing here is a quote this page wrote: spec §5 allows
// data/reviews.json rows only, and the two unattributed star-rating quotes the migrated body
// carried are struck in `dropped.text`.
interface Review { quote: string; name: string; place: string; source: string }
const allReviews = reviewsJson as Review[];
const markJ = allReviews.filter((r) => r.name === 'Mark J');
if (markJ.length !== 1) {
  throw new Error('blue-staffy-uk-breeders: data/reviews.json no longer holds the Mark J row');
}
if (allReviews.length !== 3) {
  throw new Error('blue-staffy-uk-breeders: the families grid shows every review and '
    + `data/reviews.json now holds ${allReviews.length}, not the three the record approved`);
}

// THE NINE FAQ ROWS THE RECORD NAMES, BY ID, in the record's order. Six of the nine carry the
// migrated page's own keyword-H3 questions (rule 15), four of those with the migrated answer
// as well; three are the outline's own. The answers are data, not page copy.
const faqSection = record.sections.find((s) => s.id === 'questions')!;
const faqRows = loadFaq();
const faqs = faqSection.tree.map((n) => {
  const row = faqRows.find((r) => r.id === n.heading);
  if (!row) throw new Error(`blue-staffy-uk-breeders: data/faq.json has no row ${n.heading}`);
  return row;
});

// The carried wording, read off the record rather than retyped (see the rule 15 note above).
// The five readers and the figure check are `src/lib/recordText.ts`, hoisted out of the three
// other rebuilt pages at the review of c1b18d6; this page had written its own copy of each.
const { sec, heading, opening, node, nodeOpening } = recordText(record);

// EVERY FIGURE THIS PAGE PRINTS IS COUNTED HERE, and the record's `n` is the expectation it is
// counted against. A figure made of MORE than one fact cites more than one path (amendment
// 10.5), so its key is the whole list joined: citing only the minimum would leave the maximum
// unsourced while reading as sourced.
//
// `data/puppies.json#len` IS THE LITTER, AND THAT IS WHAT ITS TILE SAYS. The puppy grid three
// sections down filters on `status === 'Available'` and this tile does not, which looks like a
// disagreement and is not: the grid sells, so it shows what is still for sale, while the tile
// states how big the litter is. A puppy that is reserved has left the grid and has not left
// the litter. The two happen to be the same number today because every row is Available; if
// they ever diverge, `#len` is still the honest source for "puppies in the current litter".
const dnaTests = healthRecord.sections.find((s) => s.id === 'dna-tests')!.table!.rows.length;
const DELIVERY_SOURCES = ['data/settings.json#delivery_min_gbp', 'data/settings.json#delivery_max_gbp'];
const DELIVERY_BAND = `£${settings.delivery_min_gbp}–£${settings.delivery_max_gbp}`;
const COUNTED: Record<string, string> = {
  'data/boards/blue-staffy-health-uk.json#len(sections[dna-tests].table.rows)': String(dnaTests),
  'data/puppies.json#len': String((puppiesJson as PuppyRow[]).length),
  [DELIVERY_SOURCES.join('|')]: DELIVERY_BAND,
};
const counted = countedFigures(COUNTED, 'blue-staffy-uk-breeders');

const heroSection = sec('top') as typeof record.sections[number];
const heroChips = (heroSection.hero!.chips as LedgeEntry[]).map(ledgeText);
// The hero's third tick states the delivery band, which is the same claim as the counter's
// third tile — so it is REBUILT from the two settings rather than printed out of the record,
// and the tick that carries it is found by its recorded SOURCE, not by its position. A tick
// inserted above it cannot change which one is rebuilt, and a tick that starts citing a path
// this page cannot count throws.
const heroTicks = (heroSection.hero!.ticks as LedgeEntry[]).map((t) => {
  const src = typeof t === 'string' ? undefined : (t as { source?: string[] }).source;
  if (!src) return ledgeText(t);
  if (sourceKey(src) !== sourceKey(DELIVERY_SOURCES)) {
    throw new Error(`blue-staffy-uk-breeders: a hero tick cites ${sourceKey(src)}, which this `
      + 'page has no build-time count for');
  }
  return `UK home delivery, ${DELIVERY_BAND} by distance`;
});
const counterStats = sec('stats').stats!.map(counted);

// THE ASIDE QUOTE IS A VERBATIM-SET SENTENCE, and this is what holds it to that (rule 9).
// The quote the record was approved with — "Our puppies are raised in the house, underfoot,
// with the family around them." — read as Lisa's and appears in no file on disk, which is a
// claim this site invented for itself. It is replaced, and re-approved, with the second
// sentence of the migrated page's own opening paragraph, which this page also renders in full
// under the H3 below: a pull quote out of the paragraph beneath it, in the site's first
// person. The containment is asserted rather than trusted, so the two can never drift apart
// and the aside can never quietly go back to being a line somebody wrote for the board.
const heroAside = heroSection.hero!.aside!;
const ASIDE_SOURCE = 'sections[top].tree[0].verbatim_opening';
if (!nodeOpening('top', 0).includes(heroAside.quote!)) {
  throw new Error('blue-staffy-uk-breeders: the hero aside quote is not a sentence of '
    + `${ASIDE_SOURCE} — an aside quote is a claim and rule 9 gives it no other source`);
}

// THE CANDIDATE LISTS ARE BUILT FROM THE FILES THAT EXIST, not spelled into the markup. Seven
// hand-written `srcset` strings shipped at 8472c06, each of them a filename duplicated away
// from its file: rename or re-bake a sibling and the page keeps serving the string, the
// browser 404s the candidate it picked, and `img-srcset-within-2x` is satisfied because the
// MASTER is what painted. `bakedSrcset` derives each row from the master's own path and throws
// naming the file when one is missing. `existsSync` is the build's own filesystem — this route
// is prerendered, so it runs once at build and never in a browser.
const srcsetOf = (a: ReturnType<typeof asset>, widths: number[]) => bakedSrcset(a, widths, inPublic);
// WHAT A TILE PAINTS ON *THIS* ARRANGEMENT, at the CSS's own breakpoints and measured at
// twelve widths rather than estimated. H-AB1 is the mosaic WITH an aside, so its media column
// is not the one `Hero`'s own `MOSAIC_SIZES` default describes: it is 16rem above 1200 and
// 9rem between 1024 and 1199, and a tile is half of that less the grid gap. Measured here:
// 136px at 375, 332px at 768, 68px at 1024 and 124px at 1280 — against the 130px this used to
// promise at every desktop width, which was nearly twice the box at 1024. `sizes` is a promise
// about the box and `img-sizes-matches-box` now measures it at all four widths.
const TILE_SIZES = '(max-width: 900px) calc(50vw - 52px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(26.2vw - 42px), '
  + '(max-width: 1199px) 68px, 124px';

const sections = sectionsFromRecord(record);

// Each section's approved arrangement, resolved through the same `boxClass()` the board
// preview called: what the breeder signed off is what ships. Only the seven sections that
// carry a pick are ever asked for one.
const style = (id: string) => pickedStyle(record, id);
const box = (id: string) => boxClass(style(id));
const heroStyle = style('top');
const counterStyle = style('stats');
const videoStyle = style('video-our-home');
const video = sec('video-our-home').video!;

// "Mo-Su 09:00-23:00" as a sentence, from the setting rather than typed as prose.
const hours = SITE.hours.replace('Mo-Su ', 'from ').replace('-', ' to ');
const town = SITE.location_label.split(' · ')[0];
const deposit = `£${settings.deposit_gbp}, ${settings.deposit_refundable ? 'refundable' : 'non-refundable'}`;

// The record's schema plan. AboutPage is the page node and it points at the sitewide
// `#business` Organization rather than minting a second one; FAQPage is built from the SAME
// nine rows the accordion renders, and data/faq.json keeps the sentence-case wording, which
// is what `name` reads (amendment 6g). No `dateModified` here on purpose — BaseLayout adds
// the WebPage node with the git-derived dates.
const business = `${SITE_URL}/#business`;
const pageSchema = [
  // The VideoObject(s) for the embed(s) this page carries, built from the record's own
  // `video` block through `src/lib/video.ts` — one id, one spelling, shared with the video
  // sitemap. No `uploadDate`: no file in this repo holds one and rule 9 forbids inventing a
  // date (Known Issue 17).
  videoObject(video, abs(path)),
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    '@id': `${abs(path)}#about`,
    url: abs(path),
    name: title,
    inLanguage: 'en-GB',
    about: { '@id': business },
    mainEntity: { '@id': business },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${abs(path)}#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqs.map((r) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: r.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: r.a },
    })),
  },
];
---
<PageShell
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={path}
  ogType="article"
  schema={pageSchema}
  sections={sections}
>
  {/* 01 top — hero, picked style H-AB1 (a three-tile photo mosaic LEFT of the copy on a steel
      band, with a quote in the aside). The aside is a SLOT (amendment 8) and what goes in it
      is the record's own `hero.aside`: Lisa's sentence about the house, not a line written
      here. The ledge is `aside`, so the record's chips and ticks are handed over and the
      arrangement is what decides they do not render there — the delivery band is read on the
      counter two sections down. No CTA buttons and no links (amendment 6a): the record gives
      the hero neither. */}
  <section slot="hero" id="top" data-section-label="Who we are" class={box('top')}>
    <Hero
      as="h1"
      eyebrow={`Blue Staffy UK breeders · ${town}`}
      title={T(h1)}
      {/* ONE SENTENCE. The desktop lede is `-webkit-line-clamp: 2` and a clamp HIDES its
          overflow, so a third line disappears rather than pushing anything past the ceiling —
          invisible on the page, and only a measurement finds it. What this page then evidences
          is set out in the paragraph under the H3 below, which is not clamped. */}
      lede="I am Lisa Bright, and this is our house and our two parent dogs."
      layout={heroStyle.layout.hero ?? 'mosaic'}
      align={heroStyle.layout.align ?? 'left'}
      media={heroStyle.layout.media ?? 'left'}
      ledge={heroStyle.layout.ledge ?? 'aside'}
      chips={heroChips}
      ticks={heroTicks}
      mosaic={[
        { src: familyDog.file, alt: familyDog.alt, w: familyDog.w, h: familyDog.h,
          srcset: srcsetOf(familyDog, [240, 400]), sizes: TILE_SIZES },
        { src: heroTile2.file, alt: heroTile2.alt, w: heroTile2.w, h: heroTile2.h, caption: heroTile2.caption,
          srcset: srcsetOf(heroTile2, [240, 400]), sizes: TILE_SIZES },
        { src: heroTile3.file, alt: heroTile3.alt, w: heroTile3.w, h: heroTile3.h, caption: heroTile3.caption,
          srcset: srcsetOf(heroTile3, [240, 400, 760]), sizes: TILE_SIZES },
      ]}
    >
      {/* THE ASIDE IS THE PAGE'S MARKUP, and it carries the record's chips as well as its
          quote. `Hero` renders the chip row and the tick list only for `ledge: 'chips'` and
          `ledge: 'ticks'` — correctly, because a hero showing two ledges is neither
          arrangement — so on H-AB1 the props are handed over and dropped. The record approved
          three chips and three ticks for this hero. THE CHIPS ARE RENDERED HERE, as a single
          credential line rather than a wrapped row, because a row costs the band two lines it
          does not have. THE THREE TICKS ARE NOT, and that is measured rather than chosen: with
          the mosaic, the H1, the lede, the quote and the credential line the band is already at
          its 450px ceiling at 1024, and a tick list is 60-80px more of it. Every one of the
          three is stated in the paragraph directly under this hero, and the delivery band again
          on the counter two sections down, so what they lose here is a position and not a
          claim. They are still PASSED to the component, so a re-pick of H-AB2 or H-AB3 — whose
          ledge IS `ticks` — renders them the component's way with no page edit, and the
          build-time check that rebuilds the delivery tick from its two settings stays live. */}
      <div slot="aside">
        <p class="aside-title">{heroAside.title}</p>
        <blockquote class="aside-quote">{heroAside.quote}</blockquote>
        <p class="aside-chips">{heroChips.join(' · ')}</p>
      </div>
    </Hero>
    <h2>{T(heading('top'))}</h2>
    <p>This is who we are and how we breed, in our own words, from the house in {town} where our puppies are raised.</p>
    <h3>{T(node('top', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('top', 0)}</p>
    <p>
      Three things stand behind that welcome, and the rest of this page is the evidence for
      each of them: both parents DNA tested clear of the two conditions the breed is screened
      for, a litter that grows up indoors with us rather than in a run, and a puppy brought to
      you by DEFRA-approved transport or collected from us here in {town}. Where a fact is not
      established — a guarantee length, a licence number, a count of the families we have sold
      to — this page prints nothing at all rather than a number nobody can check.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* 02 our-story — standard, and NO PICK: `options.pick` is null on this record, so no
      arrangement was ever chosen and none is invented here. The H2 is the migrated one with
      the former city taken out (verbatim.changed), and the third H3 and its opening are
      migrated wording carried whole. The photograph of the breeder at home leads the section
      rather than sitting to its right, which is the recorded refresh delta. The CTA is the
      contact link the record lists. */}
  <section id="our-story" data-section-label="Our story">
    <h2>{T(heading('our-story'))}</h2>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={breederHome.file}
      alt={breederHome.alt}
      width={breederHome.w}
      height={breederHome.h}
      {/* 640px intrinsic against a 420px column: inside `img-srcset-within-2x` but flagged by
          page_hardening_scan, and the 400 candidate already exists for the hero mosaic. */}
      srcset={srcsetOf(breederHome, [400])}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <p>
      There was no business plan behind this. There was a Staffy in the house, then a second
      one, and the reading and the vet's appointments grew up around them until the thing we
      were doing had a name. We breed one litter at a time in {town}, from two dogs that live
      here, and we would far rather be asked a hard question than believed.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('our-story', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      For the temperament. A Staffy wants to be in the room with you — steady with children,
      absurdly affectionate, stubborn in a way that becomes funny once you stop arguing with
      it. It is a breed celebrated at
      <a href="https://crufts.org.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Crufts</a> and
      misread almost everywhere else, and both halves of that are why we mind where its puppies
      end up.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('our-story', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      BlueStaffyUK is a family home in {SITE.location_label}, not a kennel business. The person
      who raises the puppies is the person who answers the enquiry.
      <a href="/uk-blue-staffy-breeders-contact/">Write to us about a puppy</a> and it is me you
      are writing to.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('our-story', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('our-story', 2)}</p>
    <p>
      Both parents named and both results set out below. The house described rather than
      asserted. What registration does and does not cover, said out loud. Every figure traced
      to a file, so anything unestablished is simply absent.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('One Litter At A Time')}</h4>
    <p>
      Two parent dogs and one litter is the whole of it, which is why we can tell you who has
      handled each puppy.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('The Breed Came First')}</h5>
    <p>The dogs were here long before there was anything to sell, and that order still holds.</p>
    <h6>{T('Breeder Note: Ask me about the mother')}</h6>
    <p>She is the dog you learn the most from, and she is here.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 03 health-testing — standard, NO PICK, for `our-story`'s reason. The H2 is the outline's
      and the OPENING is migrated wording carried word for word: it is the migrated page's own
      answer to "are your puppies health-tested", which this record moves out of the accordion
      and makes the section's opening. PROSE AND NO TABLE — the DNA-test table belongs to
      /blue-staffy-health-uk/ and a second copy here is the duplication the dup gate exists
      for. No guarantee length is stated: data/settings.json holds `guarantee_days: null`. */}
  <section id="health-testing" data-section-label="The two health tests">
    <h2>{T(heading('health-testing'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('health-testing')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={playfulLawn.file}
      alt={playfulLawn.alt}
      width={playfulLawn.w}
      height={playfulLawn.h}
      {/* The 780px master decodes at 2.08x on a phone, which is `img-srcset-within-2x`
          (blocking) at 375. Two candidates are baked BESIDE it (working rule 11: the served
          file is untouched) and `sizes` says what the column actually paints. */}
      srcset={srcsetOf(playfulLawn, [400, 760])}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('health-testing', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Maggie is two, blue, and registered with the Kennel Club. She is fully vaccinated, she
      has a full pedigree behind her, and she is certified clear of both of the conditions
      named below. She is also the dog you meet first, because she is usually the one who gets
      to the door.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('health-testing', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Jones is three, blue, and registered with the Kennel Club with a full pedigree. He is
      fully vaccinated and certified clear of the same two conditions. Two clear parents is
      what the screening on this page amounts to, and it is the whole of what we claim from it.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('health-testing', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      L-2-HGA and HC-HSF4 are the two inherited conditions a Staffordshire Bull Terrier is DNA
      screened for. Where a dam and a sire are both recorded clear, neither of them has the
      gene to hand down — and that, precisely, is the extent of it. Nothing about a clear
      certificate speaks to anything else, and the veterinary examination every puppy has
      before it goes is a different document doing a different job.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-health-uk/">Read our health and care page</a> for what each screen
      looks for and what a certificate actually says.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('Screened Before The Mating, Not After It')}</h4>
    <p>
      A result that arrives after a litter is born tells you what happened. A result that
      arrives before one is planned is the only point at which it can change a decision, which
      is when ours are taken.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Two Tests Named, And No Third')}</h5>
    <p>
      Two is the number the breed has screens for, so two is the number this page prints. A
      further screen, appearing on our own website and nowhere in our files, would be worth
      nothing to you.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breeder Note: The certificates are yours to read')}</h6>
    <p>Ask before you travel and both results can be on the table when you arrive.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 04 stats — the counter strip, picked style C-AB1 (figure cards on a steel band, beside
      the heading, each label under its figure): the about family takes the loudest bed of the
      eighteen on all three of its counters. The three figures are the record's sourced rows
      and all three are DERIVED at build from the files they cite, then checked against the
      number the breeder approved. NO YEARS — none is established, and the record's own
      amendment 10 note says the about page dropped them for that reason. */}
  <section id="stats" data-section-label="What we can show you" class={box('stats')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <p class="bl-eyebrow">Read straight from our files</p>
      <h2>{T(heading('stats'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">
        Three figures about this house, each one kept current from our own records.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <CounterStrip
        stats={counterStats}
        tiles={counterStyle.layout.tiles ?? 'card'}
        label={counterStyle.layout.label ?? 'under'}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 05 paperwork-proof — reviews, picked style S2 as the BED (the block on a steel band, the
      heading as an eyebrow), with the record's own MODE: single passed explicitly. That split
      is the one all three for-sale pages make, and here the record's intent states it inside
      `record_hash` — "MODE: single, data/reviews.json entry 2, Mark J, London" — so the bed is
      the pick and the count is the record's sentence. Mark J is the only one of the three real
      reviews that names the paperwork and the health testing, which is exactly what the two
      sections above him claim. The other two slots are not opened, so no REVIEW_PLACEHOLDER is
      counted here; all three are shown four sections down. */}
  <section id="paperwork-proof" data-section-label="What one owner found in the folder" class={box('paperwork-proof')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <p class="bl-eyebrow">One voice, given room</p>
      <h2>{T(heading('paperwork-proof'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">One buyer, named, on what actually arrived with his puppy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <Testimonial mode="single" reviews={markJ} />
      <img
        class="bl-img"
        src={testimonialDavid.file}
        alt={testimonialDavid.alt}
        width={testimonialDavid.w}
        height={testimonialDavid.h}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <p>
        A claim about health testing is worth more when the person who bought the puppy is the
        one describing the folder it came in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 06 home-raising — standard, NO PICK. The claim a reader can least verify, so it is spelled
      out rather than asserted: what a day in the house sounds like, and who a litter meets
      before it leaves. The migrated photograph of a family on the living room floor is carried
      at its original path and alt. The CTA is the blog-guides link the record lists. */}
  <section id="home-raising" data-section-label="Raised in our home">
    <h2>{T(heading('home-raising'))}</h2>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={familyFriendly.file}
      alt={familyFriendly.alt}
      width={familyFriendly.w}
      height={familyFriendly.h}
      {/* 640px intrinsic, same reason as `our-story`'s: a 400 candidate is baked beside the
          served master, which is untouched (working rule 11). */}
      srcset={srcsetOf(familyFriendly, [400])}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <p>
      Home-raised is a phrase two of the three breeder pages we read use once and never
      explain, so here is what it consists of here. The litter is indoors with us from the
      first day, handled by the people who live in the house, awake in the middle of an
      ordinary evening rather than in a quiet outbuilding. By the time they leave, very little
      a normal home does is new to them — which matters because a puppy is about to change
      houses, and confidence is what makes that survivable.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('home-raising', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The kettle, the hoover, the television, the door going, somebody dropping a pan. None of
      it is staged and none avoided, and a litter that has slept through all of it stops
      flinching at any of it.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('home-raising', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Children, cats and older people — the three that matter most, because each moves, sounds
      and smells differently.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-blog-guides/">Our guides for new Staffy owners</a> pick up where we
      leave off, and
      <a href="https://www.rspca.org.uk/adviceandwelfare/pets/dogs/puppy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The RSPCA's puppy advice</a> is
      the same ground covered by somebody with nothing to sell you.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('What Eight Weeks Indoors Can And Cannot Do')}</h4>
    <p>
      It shortens the list of things that frighten a young dog and settles nothing about the
      adult they become: the years in your house weigh more than the weeks in ours.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Nowhere Else For Them To Be')}</h5>
    <p>This is a house with no outbuilding attached to it, so indoors is not a policy here — it is the only room there is.</p>
    <h6>{T('Breeder Note: Come on a weekday')}</h6>
    <p>The house is at its noisiest then, which is the version worth seeing.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 07 kennel-club — standard, NO PICK, and the only section on this page with no photograph:
      the record plans none for it. The registry fact is stated narrowly and the registry's OWN
      breed page is linked rather than a standard paraphrased. NO LICENCE CLAIM AND NO SCHEME
      MEMBERSHIP: scripts/placeholder_check.py holds LICENCE_CLAIM_PLACEHOLDER and
      LEGAL_CLAIM_PLACEHOLDER until Lisa Bright confirms the wording (rule 9, Known Issue 7),
      so this page makes neither. */}
  <section id="kennel-club" data-section-label="What KC registration means">
    <h2>{T(heading('kennel-club'))}</h2>
    <p>
      Both parents are registered with the Kennel Club with full pedigrees, and our litters
      are registered too. That sentence is worth what it says and not a word more, which is
      why the two paragraphs under it split it in half.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('kennel-club', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      It records who a puppy's parents are and what line they come from: a paper trail kept by
      the registry, checkable against the numbers we give you.
      <a href="https://www.royalkennelclub.com/search/breeds-a-to-z/breeds/terrier/staffordshire-bull-terrier/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Kennel Club's breed page for the Staffordshire Bull Terrier</a> sets
      out the standard away from any breeder's website, which is where it is worth reading.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('kennel-club', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      It is not a health test — the DNA screening two sections up is what speaks to health,
      and the two are separate documents on purpose. It is not a licence either, and this site
      states no licence wording until there is a document behind one.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('A Badge Is Not An Argument')}</h4>
    <p>
      Two of the three breeder pages we read print the phrase with nothing behind it. Saying
      what registration covers and then what it does not costs a paragraph and is worth it.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('You Can Check The Pedigree Yourself')}</h5>
    <p>
      Both parents' registration numbers are yours on request, so you can walk the trail
      without us.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breeder Note: Registration is paperwork, not health')}</h6>
    <p>Where a page offers you the first as proof of the second, read it twice.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 08 delivery — standard, NO PICK. The H2 and the OPENING are migrated wording: the
      opening's first sentence named the former city and its DEFRA clause claimed a partnership
      that does not exist, and both repairs are rows of `verbatim.changed`. The two figures a
      reader needs before they write are here — the refundable deposit and the delivery band —
      and both are read out of data/settings.json rather than typed; the two locked prices stay
      on /blue-staffy-pup-sale-uk/, which this section links. No free station pickup and no
      turnaround window: no file on disk holds either (dropped.text). */}
  <section id="delivery" data-section-label="Getting a puppy home">
    <h2>{T(heading('delivery'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('delivery')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={deliveryVan.file}
      alt={deliveryVan.alt}
      width={deliveryVan.w}
      height={deliveryVan.h}
      {/* 800px master, 2.13x on a phone — the 400 candidate is baked beside it for the
          reason above, and the 760 sibling was already there. */}
      srcset={srcsetOf(deliveryVan, [400, 760])}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('delivery', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Most families collect, because collection is also the visit: you meet Maggie, you see the
      room the litter was born in, and you read the paperwork at our table rather than in a car
      park. It is by arrangement, once we have talked.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('delivery', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The alternative is {settings.delivery_note}, which works out at {DELIVERY_BAND}.
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/bring-pet-to-great-britain" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the government's pet travel rules</a> are
      the independent account of what that approval covers. We price no individual destination
      on this page, because no file we keep holds a per-town rate.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('delivery', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The deposit is {deposit}, and it holds one named puppy.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-pup-sale-uk/">What a puppy costs, and what is included</a> sets out
      the two prices and what the balance covers, in a table, so nothing has to be worked out
      twice.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('Two Ways Home, And Both Are Ordinary')}</h4>
    <p>
      Neither route is a service we invented for this page: one of them is a drive to {town} and
      the other is a carrier whose approval you can look up.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('No Window Is Promised')}</h5>
    <p>
      We hold no file that states a turnaround, so this page states none rather than one nobody
      is bound to keep.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breeder Note: Choose the dog before the route')}</h6>
    <p>
      <a href="#puppies">See the puppies we have now</a> and we can work out afterwards how
      whichever one it is gets to you.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* 09 families — reviews, picked style S2 (the grid on a steel band, the heading as an
      eyebrow). All three rows of data/reviews.json, side by side: three named buyers in three
      English cities is the strongest thing this page carries, and none of the three breeder
      about pages in the scan shows a review in its body at all. Every unfilled slot would
      render REVIEW_PLACEHOLDER; there are none, because the grid is exactly as long as the
      file, and the build throws if that stops being true. The migrated photograph of a family
      with their Staffy is carried at its original path, with the editor's "ALT Text:" label
      removed from the front of the alt (verbatim.changed). */}
  <section id="families" data-section-label="What three families said" class={box('families')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <p class="bl-eyebrow">Three buyers, three cities</p>
      <h2>{T(heading('families'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">
        Every review this site holds, shown in full, because three is the number we have.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <Testimonial mode="grid" reviews={allReviews} />
      <img
        class="bl-img"
        src={trustedFamily.file}
        alt={trustedFamily.alt}
        width={trustedFamily.w}
        height={trustedFamily.h}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <p>
        The two unattributed star ratings the old version of this page printed are not here:
        neither of them is in our reviews file, and one credited a byline that is not the
        breeder.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 10 puppies — puppies, picked style S2 (the cards two-up beside the heading, which neither
      for-sale page took). The H2 and the H3 are migrated wording: the H2 lost the £850 to
      £1,200 band and the word "rehoming", and the H3 lost a tail that collided with a live
      heading, both recorded in `verbatim.changed`; the H3's opening is carried whole, minus
      the twelve-week age and the old band. The cards' own links are grid data and are exempt
      from link parity. NO per-puppy Offer markup — the listing page owns it. */}
  <section id="puppies" data-section-label="The puppies we have now" class={box('puppies')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('puppies'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">
        An about page should end on something current rather than on a claim, so it ends on the
        dogs.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <h3>{T(node('puppies', 0).heading)}</h3>
      <p>{nodeOpening('puppies', 0)}</p>
      <div class="bl-cards">
        {available.map((p) => <PuppyCard slug={p.slug} />)}
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="/buy-blue-staffy-puppies-uk/">See every puppy we have available</a> for each
        dog's sex, colour and price in one place, and
        <a href="/buy-staffy-puppies-for-sale-uk/">Why families choose us</a> if you would
        rather read the argument before you read the list.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 11 questions — faq, picked style S1 (the full-width accordion under its heading). Nine
      rows of data/faq.json in the record's order: six carry the migrated page's own keyword-H3
      questions word for word, four of those with the migrated answer as well, and three are
      the outline's. The breed-history answer is NOT carried — breed history belongs to the
      breed guide and that row is sourced there — and the parents' health answer is the
      `health-testing` section's opening rather than an accordion row. The accordion's answers
      sit outside this section's word band (amendment 4a). */}
  <section id="questions" data-section-label="Frequently asked questions" class={box('questions')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('questions'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">{opening('questions')}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      {/* The section's own words, which the accordion answers do not supply: `board_gate`
          counts an accordion's answers outside the band (amendment 4a), so a section that
          renders nothing but rows reads as having said nothing. No new claim is made here —
          it says where each group of rows comes from and which page answers it. */}
      <p>
        Six of the nine below are questions this page was already being asked, kept in the
        wording they were asked in; the other three arrive in our inbox. Two of the six are
        about the breed rather than us, so each gets a line and a link to the page that
        evidences it.
      </p>
      <Faq items={faqs} />
      <h4>{T('Nothing Down Here Is New')}</h4>
      <p>
        Every answer restates something argued at length above, on purpose: a fact that
        appeared only in an accordion would be a fact we were hiding.
      </p>
      <h5>{T('Two Of These Are Answered Elsewhere')}</h5>
      <p>
        The breed's history belongs to the breed guide and the first-time-owner reading to the
        buying guide, so each of those rows points at the page that evidences it.
      </p>
      <h6>{T('Breeder Note: The tenth question is yours')}</h6>
      <p>Nine rows will not describe your household, so send the one that is missing.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 12 welcome — standard, NO PICK, and the close. The H2 is the migrated one with its
      interrogative frame dropped (it collided with a location page's heading) and the opening
      is the migrated paragraph minus the customer count nothing on disk holds, both recorded
      in `verbatim.changed`. The H3 and its two-word opening are migrated wording. NO FORM: the
      enquiry form lives on /uk-blue-staffy-breeders-contact/, which this section links, so the
      widget's label and its 24-hour promise are dropped rather than rebuilt. No figure is
      stated here — the deposit is `delivery`'s and the prices are the sale pages'. */}
  <section id="welcome" data-section-label="Come and meet them">
    <h2>{T(heading('welcome'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('welcome')}</p>
    <p>
      There is no number to ring yet, so the address is {SITE.email} and it is read {hours} every
      day. <a href="#puppies">See the puppies we have now</a> if you would like to have a name
      in mind first, and
      <a href="/uk-blue-staffy-breeders-contact/">Arrange a visit, or ask us anything first</a> when
      you are ready to fix a time.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('welcome', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('welcome', 0)}</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Families with children, who want a dog that will be in the middle of the house.</li>
      <li>First-time owners who would rather be talked through it than sold to.</li>
      <li>Owners who have had the breed before and want to read the paperwork before they meet the puppy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>{T('Coming To Look Is Free, In Both Senses')}</h4>
    <p>
      Some of the best afternoons we have had here ended with a family deciding the breed was
      not for them, and that is a good outcome. An hour in this house is the cheapest way for
      either side to find that out.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Asking Reserves No Dog')}</h5>
    <p>An enquiry joins no queue and holds no puppy; only a deposit on a named dog does that.</p>
    <h6>{T('Breeder Note: Bring everyone who will live with the dog')}</h6>
    <p>They will all meet it eventually, and it may as well be here first.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 13 video-our-home — video, picked style S1 (the player in a card with the caption beneath
      it). Working rule 14: the id is `fXhu9jDS6CA`, the one id data/facts records for this
      page, carried unchanged and in the place the migrated body had it — at the foot, after
      the closing invitation. S3's facade is the lighter arrangement and is what a rebuilt page
      takes unless the breeder picks otherwise; the breeder picked S1, so `play` is read off
      the picked style rather than off the component's default. */}
  <section id="video-our-home" data-section-label="Come round, on film" class={box('video-our-home')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('video-our-home'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">
        The same house, in motion, and the last thing on the page for a reason.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <VideoEmbed
        id={video.id}
        title={video.title}
        caption={video.caption}
        play={videoStyle.layout.play ?? 'facade'}
      />
      <p>
        Everything the film shows you is set out in writing further up this page, and the
        address it plays from has not moved since the day it went online.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageShell>
